## 3. Why source power matters economically

One way to deliver the same dose faster is to generate more usable EUV light.

More source power means the scanner can put the required energy onto the wafer in less time.

That is why source power is not merely an engineering specification. It can become capacity.

As of April 2026, ASML said the NXE:3800E had reached 230 wafers per hour, up from 220 wafers per hour, and that its longer-term Low-NA EUV roadmap targets at least 330 wafers per hour around the start of the next decade.

Going from 220 to 230 wafers per hour is roughly a 4.5% increase in nominal throughput from an installed tool.

For equipment this expensive and difficult to add quickly, a few percentage points of productivity can matter.

## 4. Scanner count is not the same as capacity

Imagine two fabs each own 20 EUV scanners.

Fab A runs lower-dose recipes, has excellent uptime and uses 15 EUV layers per wafer.

Fab B runs more difficult, higher-dose recipes, has more downtime and uses 20 EUV layers per wafer.

The two fabs do not have the same usable EUV capacity even though the machine count is identical.

This is why a statement such as “the fab installed five more EUV tools” is incomplete.

You also need to know:

- qualified wafers per hour;
- required dose;
- tool availability;
- utilization;
- EUV layers per wafer;
- and whether EUV is actually the marginal bottleneck.

## 5. One wafer can consume EUV capacity many times

A leading-edge wafer does not pass through EUV once.

Different critical layers require separate exposures.

So if a process moves from 15 EUV layers to 20, the same wafer-start volume creates about one-third more EUV exposure demand before considering any change in dose.

That is **lithography intensity**.

This connects directly to multiple patterning. Manufacturing demand depends on the number and difficulty of process passes, not simply on the number of wafers entering the fab.

## 6. Better resist can act like hidden capacity

Now reconnect this to Lesson 019.

Suppose a better resist achieves the same pattern quality at a lower dose.

The resist itself may cost more, but if it lets the scanner expose wafers faster without increasing stochastic defects, the fab can get more output from the installed EUV fleet.

So a materials improvement can behave like a small capacity expansion.

The chain is:

better resist or more source power → acceptable pattern with less exposure time → more wafers per hour → more effective EUV capacity

That is why tool vendors, materials suppliers and process integration cannot be analyzed independently.

